## What I learned
Data can arrive in different ways, such as batch and streaming, depending on how close to real-time the data needs to be. More real-time data usually requires more resources, so there are trade-offs and costs involved.

Incremental loading has three fundamental patterns: append, overwrite, and merge/upsert. The appropriate pattern depends on how the source data changes.

I learned about Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD). Type 1 overwrites old values, while Type 2 keeps historical records and indicates which record is current.

CDC (Change Data Capture) finds the changes, while SCD stores the changes for historical tracking.

Idempotency means that running the same pipeline multiple times with the same source data should produce the same result.

Understanding the grain of both the production/source data and destination tables is important when designing data pipelines.

Data can be ingested through different patterns, including database ingestion, API ingestion, and web scraping.

For APIs, I need to understand concepts such as endpoints, JSON, authentication, pagination, rate limits, request limits, errors, and timeouts.

Dates can be complicated because of different formats, GMT/time zones, and daylight saving time.

Web scraping involves understanding HTML structure, tags, opening/closing tags, and parsing. Just because data is technically accessible does not necessarily mean it is permitted to scrape or use.

Provenance and governance are important for traceability and understanding where data came from.

A modular pipeline can help organize and maintain data processing more effectively.

I should learn to use Python locally because it will be useful for working with APIs, parsing data, and building pipelines.

